m cited studies?\n- Are literature citations present in reaction reasoning?\n- Does the network acknowledge critiques and known challenges identified in the research?\n- Departures from the research are acceptable when explicitly and soundly justified.\n\n## Scoring Guidelines\n- 0.9-1.0: Excellent network, valid and chemically sound\n- 0.8-0.9: Good network with minor issues\n- 0.7-0.8: Acceptable but with notable concerns\n- 0.5-0.7: Significant problems with validity or chemistry\n- 0.0-0.5: Major issues, network is not viable\n\nA network is APPROVED if overall_confidence >= 0.9\n\nOutput your evaluation as JSON matching the NetworkEvaluation schema.\n", "user_message": "Evaluate this synthesis network:\n\nTARGET MOLECULE: L-Proline\nOVERALL STRATEGY: Experimentally grounded synthesis is anchored on the surface cyanosulfidic sequence of Patel et al. (2015), in which 3-aminopropanal is converted to rac-proline by cyanohydrin formation, H2S-driven thiolation-cyclization, Cu0/H2S reductive deoxygenation, thione-to-nitrile exchange, and nitrile hydrolysis. The network therefore focuses on surface chemistry and supplies 3-aminopropanal through three chemically plausible feeder channels: direct acrolein amination, glycerol dehydration followed by amination, or net reductive amination of malondialdehyde. Because the validated chemistry is racemic rather than asymmetric, the requested L-proline target is interpreted as an L-enriched pool reached by explicitly speculative post-synthetic enrichment at biochemical mineral interfaces or by asymmetric crystallization. High-temperature hydrothermal routes are not emphasized because the key amino aldehyde and aminonitrile intermediates are better supported and likely more persistent in milder surface pools. Ten representative pathways are enumerated from these branches.\n\nMOLECULES (15 nodes):\n  - mol_001: HCN (hydrogen cyanide) [starting]\n  - mol_002: H2S (hydrogen sulfide) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_003: H2O (water) [hub