"target\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Thermal dehydration of glycerol to acrolein\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"340-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": false,\n        \"other\": \"acidic geothermal drying films or evaporitic crusts under anoxic conditions\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Heat-driven dehydration of glycerol through hydroxyaldehyde or enol-like intermediates removes two waters overall and yields acrolein.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative feeder. The curated review identifies acrolein as a plausible upstream source of 3-aminopropanal and notes that acrolein can arise from thermal decomposition or dehydration of glycerol in surface geothermal settings. Patel et al. (2015) did not include this feeder in the demonstrated proline sequence, so it is used here only as a chemically plausible supply reaction.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Aqueous ammonolysis of acrolein to 3-aminopropanal\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"shallow anoxic pools with moderate ammonia; short residence time to limit acrolein polymerization\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia adds conjugatively to the beta carbon of acrolein, followed by proton transfer and tautomerization to the beta-amino aldehyde 3-aminopropanal.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative feeder. The review explicitly lists acrolein plus ammonia as a plausible origin of 3-aminopropanal, which is the entry point to the cyanosulfidic proline pathway. This step is chemically reasonable in surface pools, but the concentration and stability of 3-aminopropanal remain an open constraint noted in the review (Patel et al., 2015).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Dry-down ammonolysis of acrolein to 3-aminopropanal\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"310-350K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": false,\n        \"other\": \"wet-dry cycling on evaporitic margins or tidal flats; concentrated thin films enrich ammonia and acrolein\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The same conjugate addition chemistry as rxn_002 occurs in concentrated drying films, where higher ammonia activity and lower bulk water favor rapid beta-amino aldehyde formation.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative feeder variant. Patel et al. (2015) did not separately demonstrate this condition set, but the review places the chemistry in wet-dry surface environments where concentration effects are plausible. This branch is included because acrolein ammonolysis should remain chemically viable during dry-down even though side reactions and product instability are likely.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Sulfidic reductive amination of malondialdehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_001\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"sulfidic anoxic pools; the written net step formalizes imine reduction by sulfide\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"One aldehyde of malondialdehyde condenses with ammonia to an imine or iminium, and sulfide supplies reducing equivalents to give the amino aldehyde while sulfur is oxidized formally to elemental sulfur.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative feeder. The curated review notes reductive amination of malondialdehyde as another possible origin of 3-aminopropanal. The exact prebiotic implementation is unresolved, so this node encodes a sulfide-driven net transformation consistent with anoxic sulfur-rich chemistry rather than a directly demonstrated step; sulfur is written explicitly as the oxidized coproduct so the reaction remains balanced (Patel et al., 2015).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Strecker-type cyanohydrin formation from 3-aminopropanal\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"290-310K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"cyanide-bearing surface water under anoxic cyanosulfidic conditions\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanide adds to the aldehyde carbonyl of 3-aminopropanal to form the alpha-hydroxynitrile 4-amino-2-hydroxybutanenitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Experimentally demonstrated entry to proline chemistry. Patel et al. (2015) showed HCN addition to 3-aminopropanal to give this aminonitrile cyanohydrin. This is the key C-C bond-forming step that sets the chain length required for five-membered ring closure (Patel et al., 2015).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"H2S-mediated thiolation-cyclization to 3-hydroxypyrrolidine-2-thione\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"290-320K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"sulfidic atmosphere or dissolved sulfide in shallow pools\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrogen sulfide converts the nitrile into a thioamide-like center, after which the terminal amine attacks intramolecularly to close the pyrrolidine ring and release ammonia.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Experimentally demonstrated. Patel et al. (2015) showed that H2S converts the aminonitrile into the first cyclic intermediate, 3-hydroxypyrrolidine-2-thione. This is the ring-forming transformation that makes the proline route distinctive relative to conventional Strecker synthesis (Patel et al., 2015).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Cu and sulfide reductive deoxygenation to pyrrolidine-2-thione\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_001\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"native copper (Cu0)\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"290-320K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"native copper (Cu0)\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"sulfidic surface pools or mineral films; Cu0 grains formed by sulfide reduction of dissolved copper species\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The hydroxylated thione undergoes reductive deoxygenation in a Cu and sulfide system, giving pyrrolidine-2-thione with water formation and formal oxidation of sulfide to elemental sulfur.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) experimentally used a Cu0 and H2S system for this reductive deoxygenation. Their narrative shorthand does not specify a single sulfur oxidation product, so this network writes H2S explicitly as the stoichiometric reductant and elemental sulfur as the oxidized coproduct to keep the net step balanced. That is a bookkeeping correction rather than a claim that sulfur speciation was simple, and the review notes that Cu0 availability itself remains an open question (Patel et al., 2015).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanide-mediated desulfurative cyanation of pyrrolidine-2-thione\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"290-320K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"cyanide-rich anoxic surface water under cyanosulfidic conditions\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanide attacks the thione carbon, and sulfur is expelled as elemental sulfur while the carbonitrile functionality is installed at C2 of the pyrrolidine ring.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) demonstrated the thione-to-nitrile conversion under cyanosulfidic conditions. The common shorthand that writes H2S as the only sulfur product is not atom-balanced for the isolated step, so this network represents the immediate sulfur loss as elemental sulfur to preserve mass while retaining the experimentally supported functional-group exchange. Any later reduction of sulfur back to sulfide would require additional reductants not included here (Patel et al., 2015).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Net hydrolysis of pyrrolidine-2-carbonitrile to L-proline\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"aqueous pools with geothermal pH or temperature cycling to accelerate otherwise slow nitrile hydrolysis\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The nitrile is hydrolyzed through the usual amide manifold to the carboxylic acid; the species list leaves the required two waters to coefficient inference and ammonia is released overall.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) reported hydrolysis of pyrrolidine-2-carbonitrile to proline. The curated review notes that this final hydrolysis may be kinetically slow under ambient conditions, so mild geothermal cycling is chemically useful. The underlying chemistry is expected to be racemic; the L label here denotes the requested target rather than demonstrated stereocontrol (Patel et al., 2015).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Partial hydrolysis of pyrrolidine-2-carbonitrile to prolinamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-350K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"concentrated prebiotic pools or geothermal cycling with prolonged residence times\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Water adds to the nitrile and rearranges to the primary amide, making the mechanistically expected first stage of nitrile hydrolysis explicit.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Mechanistically grounded but not isolated as a separate proline-pathway step in Patel et al. (2015). Nitrile hydrolysis normally proceeds through the primary amide, so prolinamide is included as an explicit branch for a slower two-step terminal sequence rather than hiding the chemistry inside a net step. This is a justified extension of the reported hydrolysis chemistry (Patel et al., 2015).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolysis of prolinamide to L-proline\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-360K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"wet-dry or thermal cycling at mineral interfaces in concentrated organic pools\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The amide undergoes aqueous hydrolysis to the carboxylic acid with ammonia release, completing the conversion to proline.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Mechanistically grounded continuation of nitrile hydrolysis. Conversion of the amide to the acid with ammonia release is standard aqueous chemistry and is implicit in the net hydrolysis reported by Patel et al. (2015). As with rxn_009, this step reaches proline constitutionally but does not solve the open homochirality problem (Patel et al., 2015).\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct acrolein feeder with net hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Acrolein is taken as a surface feedstock, aminated in dilute aqueous surface water, then carried through the cyanosulfidic ring-building core and a net nitrile hydrolysis.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct acrolein feeder with amide-tracked hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"The same aqueous acrolein entry as P1 is used, but the terminal nitrile hydrolysis is expanded through prolinamide in concentrated biochemical pools.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Dry-down acrolein feeder with net hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Acrolein is aminated during surface dry-down concentration rather than in dilute water, then enters the same cyanosulfidic core and direct hydrolysis branch.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Dry-down acrolein feeder with amide-tracked hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"The dry-down acrolein ammonolysis route is coupled to the explicit amide branch for terminal hydrolysis in concentrated prebiotic pools.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Glycerol dehydration plus aqueous acrolein ammonolysis with net hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Glycerol is first dehydrated to acrolein in a hot drying film, after which the acrolein is aminated in dilute surface water and passed through the cyanosulfidic core to direct hydrolysis.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Glycerol dehydration plus aqueous acrolein ammonolysis with amide-tracked hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"The glycerol-to-acrolein feeder is combined with aqueous ammonolysis and the explicit prolinamide branch for the terminal hydrolysis.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Glycerol dehydration plus dry-down acrolein ammonolysis with net hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Both glycerol dehydration and acrolein ammonolysis occur along a surface wet-dry cycle before the standard cyanosulfidic core and direct hydrolysis complete the route.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Glycerol dehydration plus dry-down acrolein ammonolysis with amide-tracked hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"A fully surface wet-dry feeder from glycerol to 3-aminopropanal is followed by the explicit amide branch to the target.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Malondialdehyde reductive amination feeder with net hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"A speculative sulfidic reductive amination of malondialdehyde supplies 3-aminopropanal, after which the literature-backed cyanosulfidic core and direct nitrile hydrolysis follow.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Malondialdehyde reductive amination feeder with amide-tracked hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"The same speculative malondialdehyde feeder as P9 is combined with explicit prolinamide formation and hydrolysis as the terminal branch.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_014\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\"mol_003\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_013\"],\n  \"converge