r cyanosulfidic conditions. The common shorthand that writes H2S as the only sulfur product is not atom-balanced for the isolated step, so this network represents the immediate sulfur loss as elemental sulfur to preserve mass while retaining the experimentally supported functional-group exchange. Any later reduction of sulfur back to sulfide would require additional reductants not included here (Patel et al., 2015)."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Net hydrolysis of pyrrolidine-2-carbonitrile to L-proline", "inputs": ["mol_013", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_015", "mol_002"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-340K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "aqueous pools with geothermal pH or temperature cycling to accelerate otherwise slow nitrile hydrolysis"}, "mechanism": "The nitrile is hydrolyzed through the usual amide manifold to the carboxylic acid; the species list leaves the required two waters to coefficient inference and ammonia is released overall.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. (2015) reported hydrolysis of pyrrolidine-2-carbonitrile to proline. The curated review notes that this final hydrolysis may be kinetically slow under ambient conditions, so mild geothermal cycling is chemically useful. The underlying chemistry is expected to be racemic; the L label here denotes the requested target rather than demonstrated stereocontrol (Patel et al., 2015)."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Partial hydrolysis of pyrrolidine-2-carbonitrile to prolinamide", "inputs": ["mol_013", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_014"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-350K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "concentrated prebiotic pools or geothermal cycling with prolonged residence times"}, "mechanism": "Water adds to the nitrile and rearranges to the primary amide, making the mechanistically expected first stage of nitrile hydrolysis explicit.", "reasoning": "Mechanistically grounded but not isolated as a separate proline-pathway step in Patel et al. (2015). Nitrile hydrolysis normally proceeds through the primary amide, so prolinamide is included as an explicit branch for a slower two-step terminal sequence rather than hiding the chemistry inside a net step. This is a justified extension of the reported hydrolysis chemistry (Patel et al., 2015)."}, {"id": "rxn_011", "name": "Hydrolysis of prolinamide to L-proline", "inputs": ["mol_014", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_015", "mol_002"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-360K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "wet-dry or thermal cycling at mineral interfaces in concentrated organic pools"}, "mechanism": "The amide undergoes aqueous hydrolysis to the carboxylic acid with ammonia release, completing the conversion to proline.", "reasoning": "Mechanistically grounded continuation of nitrile hydrolysis. Conversion of the amide to the acid with ammonia release is standard aqueous chemistry and is implicit in the net hydrolysis reported by Patel et al. (2015). As with rxn_009, this step reaches proline constitutionally but does not solve the open homochirality problem (Patel et al., 2015)."}], "pathways": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "name": "Direct acrolein feeder with net hydrolysis", "description": "Acrolein is taken as a surface feedstock, aminated in dilute aqueous surface water, then carried through the cyanosulfidic ring-building core and a net nitrile hydrolysis.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_002", "rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_008", "rxn_009"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_009", "mol_010", "mol_013"]}, {"pathway_id": "P2", "name": "Direct acrolein feeder with amide-tracked hydrolysis", "description": "The same aqueous acrolein entry as P1 is used, but the terminal nitrile hydrolysis is expanded through prolinamide in concentrated biochemical pools.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_002", "rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_008", "rxn_010", "rxn_011"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_009", "mol_010", "mol_014"]}, {"pathway_id": "P3", "name": "Dry-down acrolein feeder with net hydrolysis", "description": "Acrolein is aminated during surface dry-down concentration rather than in dilute water, then enters the same cyanosulfidic core and direct hydrolysis branch.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_003", "rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_008", "rxn_009"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_009", "mol_012", "mol_013"]}, {"pathway_id": "P4", "name": "Dry-down acrolein feeder with amide-tracked hydrolysis", "description": "The dry-down acrolein ammonolysis route is coupled to the explicit amide branch for terminal hydrolysis in concentrated prebiotic pools.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_003", "rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_008", "rxn_010", "rxn_011"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_009", "mol_013", "mol_014"]}, {"pathway_id": "P5", "name": "Glycerol dehydration plus aqueous acrolein ammonolysis with net hydrolysis", "description": "Glycerol is first dehydrated to acrolein in a hot drying film, after which the acrolein is aminated in dilute surface water and passed through the cyanosulfidic core to direct hydrolysis.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_008", "rxn_009"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_007", "mol_009", "mol_013"]}, {"pathway_id": "P6", "name": "Glycerol dehydration plus aqueous acrolein ammonolysis with amide-tracked hydrolysis", "description": "The glycerol-to-acrolein feeder is combined with aqueous ammonolysis and the explicit prolinamide branch for the terminal hydrolysis.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_008", "rxn_010", "rxn_011"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_007", "mol_009", "mol_014"]}, {"pathway_id": "P7", "name": "Glycerol dehydration plus dry-down acrolein ammonolysis with net hydrolysis", "description": "Both glycerol dehydration and acrolein ammonol