  \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295-310K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic H2S-rich cyanosulfidic pool.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"H2S converts the nitrile into a thioamide equivalent, after which the terminal amine attacks intramolecularly to close the five-membered ring and expel ammonia.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) experimentally support this one-pot thiolation/cyclization. It is the decisive ring-forming step that makes proline accessible: the 3-aminopropanal-derived chain length is exactly suited for five-membered pyrrolidine closure, which explains the route's structural selectivity.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Cu/H2S reductive deoxygenation to pyrrolidine-2-thione\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_015\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"native copper (Cu0)\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295-310K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"native copper (Cu0)\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Aqueous sulfidic surface setting with dispersed Cu0; sulfur bookkeeping is represented as elemental sulfur.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The Cu/H2S reducing system removes the C3 hydroxyl group as water, yielding the fully reduced pyrrolidine-2-thione; sulfur is tracked as the formal oxidation sink.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) demonstrated the deoxygenation using a Cu/H2S system. In this graph, H2S is written explicitly as the reductant and sulfur as the atom-balanced oxidation sink because the exact sulfur speciation is not resolved in a coefficient-free node representation. The review correctly notes that the abundance and persistence of native Cu0 are open questions in prebiotic settings.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Thione-to-nitrile conversion to pyrrolidine-2-carbonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_015\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295-310K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"HCN-rich cyanosulfidic pool; sulfur is recorded as a neutral sulfur sink for explicit atom balance.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"HCN converts the C2 thione/thioamide functionality of the pyrrolidine ring into the corresponding nitrile-bearing carbon center, with sulfur extrusion.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) support conversion of pyrrolidine-2-thione into pyrrolidine-2-carbonitrile as part of the proline sequence. The paper discusses sulfur recycling within the cyanosulfidic network; here the coproduct is tracked as elemental sulfur rather than H2S so the graph remains explicitly atom-balanced without hidden proton/electron carriers. This is a bookkeeping choice rather than a rejection of the sulfur-cycle concept (Patel et al., 2015).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Ambient aqueous nitrile hydrolysis to proline\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Long aqueous residence time in shallow pools; two water equivalents are inferred.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Net sequential hydration of the nitrile to an amide and then to the carboxylic 